    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103",
      "id": "q-107837",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-107837",
        "knowledge_point_id": "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259",
        "knowledge_point": "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix",
        "problem": "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$",
        "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] & =7[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5c+11 \\\\ 13c-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7c+7 \\\\ 7c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5c+11=7c+7 \\\\ 13c-12=7c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c+11=7c+7\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n13c-12=7c\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=2.$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
